The app environment injector is created from allAppProviders which includes my provider. So `inject(UrlSerializer)` anywhere in the app environment should resolve to my GwtUrlSerializer...

Hmm — so why isn't it?! Let me look for `EnvironmentNgModuleRefAdapter` and whether it does something weird with providers — like processing `makeEnvironmentProviders` differently... `provideRouter(routes, withHashLocation())` returns makeEnvironmentProviders([...]) — an EnvironmentProviders wrapper. My `{provide...}` is a plain provider. Both end up in the same injector. The wrapper is just a type-safety marker (and `isEnvironmentProviders` check to REJECT it in component providers). Both fine at env level.

Hmm!!! Wait a moment — let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is not DI at all! What if my provider IS registered and the Router DOES use GwtUrlSerializer — but the counters are null because the serializer methods were called BEFORE... no, counters would still increment. Unless `globalThis` in the module scope refers to something else? `globalThis.__gwtSerialize = (globalThis.__gwtSerialize ?? 0) + 1` — globalThis is standard. If serialize ran, window.__gwtSerialize would be set. The probe reads `window.__gwtSerialize` — null.

So serialize() genuinely never ran. And the href is '#/manufacturer'.

Hmm — but WAIT. Is it possible that the ROUTER LINK href came out as '#/manufacturer' through a different mechanism: `Location.prepareExternalUrl`?? Look at RouterLink again — line 5985 area — `this.locationStrategy?.prepareExternalUrl(this.router.serializeUrl(urlTree))`. `router.serializeUrl` → `this.urlSerializer.serialize(tree)`.

And `locationStrategy` — RouterLink injects `@Optional() @Inject(LOCATION_INITIALIZED)...`? Let me look at the RouterLink constructor in 18.2: does it inject LocationStrategy or Location? If Location.prepareExternalUrl is used, it PREPENDS '/' — that would produce '#/manufacturer' EVEN WITH my serializer!!! Look:

```javascript
prepareExternalUrl(url) {
    if (url && url[0] !== '/') {
        url = '/' + url;
    }
    return this._locationStrategy.prepareExternalUrl(url);
}
```

If RouterLink's href uses `Location.prepareExternalUrl(serialize(tree))` → serialize gives 'manufacturer' → Location prepends '/' → '/manufacturer' → HashLocationStrategy → '#/manufacturer'. THAT would explain the HREF!

But then my serializer WOULD have been called (serialize) — counter set. It's null. Contradiction... UNLESS the bundle the browser ran is... wait, I confirmed the served bundle contains __gwtSerialize and I loaded main-EVJPUUUW.js. Hmm, but actually — did the browser use a CACHED old main-*.js?? The previous build was main-UBVLKVRA.js — different hash → no cache collision. index.html references EVJPUUUW ✓.

And the URL '#/manufacturer' — the initial URL was '#manufacturer' → became '#/manufacturer'. Which code did THAT? If my serializer were injected... hmm.

Let me now consider: maybe BOTH the URL rewrite AND hrefs come from `Location.go/replaceState` being called with '/manufacturer' from... the ROUTER — because the router's urlSerializer is the DEFAULT one.

OK — enough theorizing; the evidence says the Router uses the default serializer. There must be a subtlety in how `{provide: UrlSerializer}` in appConfig.providers is processed... 

WAIT!!!! I JUST REALIZED SOMETHING. Look at the minified provider: `{provide:xn,useClass:Hs}`. And `xn` declaration: `var xn=(()=>{class e{static{this.ɵprov=w({token:e,factory:()=>new Mn,providedIn:"root"})}}return e})()`.

`w` = `ɵɵngDeclareInjectable`. This creates `xn.ɵprov` with providedIn: "root".

Now — in Angular's R3Injector, when resolving a token:
1. Look in the injector's own provider defs — my provider IS there (registered when the injector was created).

Hmm, but is it? The injector is created from allAppProviders — provider defs processed... `EnvironmentNgModuleRefAdapter({providers: allAppProviders, parent: platformInjector, ...})` — creates an EnvironmentInjector with those providers.

I'm confident this normally works — people DO override UrlSerializer via `{provide: UrlSerializer, useClass: ...}` in appConfig... Actually hmm, let me search my memory: Angular docs for UrlSerializer custom:

```typescript
providers: [
  { provide: UrlSerializer, useClass: CustomUrlSerializer }
]
```
That's the documented approach with RouterModule.forRoot / provideRouter. So it SHOULD work!

...

Let me look at the minified `Eg(om, Ig())` — `Eg` = provideRouter, which returns `makeEnvironmentProviders([...])`. In the minified code, is `Eg(om,Ig())` returning a WRAPPER object `{ɵproviders: [...]}` rather than a flat array?! YES — `makeEnvironmentProviders` wraps: `function makeEnvironmentProviders(providers) { return { ɵproviders: providers }; }` hmm — actually in 18.2: 

```javascript
function makeEnvironmentProviders(providers) {
    return {
        ɵproviders: providers,
    };
}
```

Hmm no wait — that's `importProvidersFrom`... Let me check. If provideRouter returns `{ɵproviders: [...]}` and that object sits as an ELEMENT of the providers array — Angular's provider processing FLATTENS such wrappers (that's the whole point). EnvironmentInjector processes nested arrays and EnvironmentProviders wrappers.

